Continuation of the ports/analyze extraction (PRODUCT_RELEASE_PLAN_AR.md §4.1)
after link.* (docs/evidence/08-carrier-loss-fix.log). Two more families done
in this pass:

## l2.* (neighbour/ARP) - the highest-value family in the real evidence

internal/ports/neighbor.go + internal/analyze/neighbor.go, replacing the
decision logic previously inline in internal/collect/netlink/neigh_linux.go.
internal/collect/netlink/severity.go (FirstSightingSeverity) moved alongside
it into internal/analyze/severity.go - same category of pure decision logic,
same reasoning as the original file's own comment ("The policy lives apart
from the netlink plumbing so it can be tested without a kernel").

Gateway detection (is this address the current default gateway) stays in the
adapter (internal/collect/netlink/neigh_linux.go's isGateway, with its
existing 30s cache) rather than moving into the analyzer: it needs a
route-table read, which is exactly the kind of platform-specific plumbing
the analyzer must never reach for itself. The adapter computes the answer
and hands it in on the observation - the general pattern for any future
"needs another table" case.

14 tests in internal/analyze/neighbor_test.go: new binding, gateway vs
ordinary severity on both first-sighting and change, duplicate-IP detection
(three flips triggers it, two does not, changes aging out of the 60s window
do not accumulate), MAC-moved, neighbor-failed, and the boundary cases
(failure on an unknown address, an unchanged re-announcement).

## l3.* (route + address)

internal/ports/route.go + internal/analyze/route.go, replacing
internal/collect/netlink/route_linux.go's RouteCollector AND AddrCollector
logic. Route is the more complex of the two (tracks every route to a
destination, not just the winner, exactly preserving the original's
upsert/supersede/remove/winner semantics - a replace is matched by priority
alone, not by where it points, since re-pointing a prefix at a new next hop
at the same metric is precisely the common case a replace expresses).
Address is stateless (ObserveAddress is a plain function, no analyzer
struct needed - there was nothing to diff in the original either).

11 new tests: route added to a fresh destination, a worse-metric standby
NOT changing the winner, losing the winner promotes a standby (route
changed, not added+removed), withdrawing the only route, default-route
severity (error) vs ordinary-route severity (notice) on removal, default
route moving (default_route_changed, error) vs an ordinary route moving
(route_changed, notice), a replace at a different metric adding an
alternative rather than superseding, and address add/remove.

## Verification (both families, both platforms)

- `gofmt -l` clean on both.
- `go vet` clean, native and `GOOS=linux`, on Windows.
- Full repo test suite: passes on Windows (`go test ./...`) and on Linux
  with the race detector (`CGO_ENABLED=1 go test -race ./...` in the
  `netrewind-lab` WSL distro) - 43 tests in internal/analyze total after
  this pass (20 interface + 14 neighbor + 9 route/address, i.e. the
  additions), zero regressions anywhere else.
- The real lab (`lab/inject.sh all`, rebuilt Linux binaries, restaged, run
  fresh): **PASS** both times (once after neighbor, once after route/address),
  all 14 event kinds recorded and all 8 correlation rules concluded each
  time, with `link.down` staying at 2 recorded - confirming the carrier-loss
  fix from the link.* extraction survived two further unrelated refactors
  intact.

